# Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by each clone (run 0044).
#
# Measured before writing this, with `git ls-files --eol`:
#
#   308 files  i/lf w/crlf   every text file: LF in git, CRLF on disk
#     2 files  i/lf w/lf     the two Context copies under _cowork/design/landing
#    45 files  i/-text       32 .jpg, 12 .png, and PROJECT_STATE.md, which held
#                            a NUL byte until the commit before this one
#    15 files  i/none        empty files, nothing to convert
#     0 files  i/crlf        no blob in this repository holds CRLF
#
# So the repository already stores LF and checks out CRLF, which is what
# `core.autocrlf=true` on this machine has been doing. `text=auto eol=crlf`
# writes that down, and the working tree does not move: it is what the 308
# already have.
#
# The point is that it stops depending on a setting each clone makes for
# itself. A machine with `core.autocrlf=false` would commit CRLF blobs and the
# next diff would be the whole file.

* text=auto eol=crlf

# The two files above that are LF on disk today. They are byte copies of the
# shared landing fragment in the Context folder, fingerprinted by
# `src/landingShared.test.ts` against a hash the brief records, and keeping them
# as they arrived is what makes a byte comparison mean anything. The test
# normalises CRLF to LF before hashing, so this is about the copy rather than
# about the test.
_cowork/design/landing/landing.html text eol=lf
_cowork/design/landing/README.md    text eol=lf

# Pictures and other bytes git must never touch.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
